WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.630 --> 00:00:02.290
Who doesn't love Yoda?

00:00:02.290 --> 00:00:06.990
He's a little green mystical guru who rides
around in a hover chair and beats robots with

00:00:06.990 --> 00:00:07.990
sticks.

00:00:07.990 --> 00:00:11.340
Oh yeah, and he's a seasoned general, war
hero, and possibly the strongest Force user

00:00:11.340 --> 00:00:12.340
in the galaxy.

00:00:12.340 --> 00:00:16.880
But, like a lot of stuff related to Star Wars,
once you dig deeper into the movies and broader

00:00:16.880 --> 00:00:20.810
canon, you start to realize stuff you might
not have noticed at first like that this miniature

00:00:20.810 --> 00:00:23.230
Jedi master can be a huge jerk.

00:00:23.230 --> 00:00:27.199
Here's a look back at the worst things Yoda
has ever done.

00:00:27.199 --> 00:00:29.079
Doing Ahsoka wrong

00:00:29.079 --> 00:00:33.500
In The Phantom Menace, one of Yoda's concerns
about accepting Anakin Skywalker into the

00:00:33.500 --> 00:00:38.130
Jedi Order is that he's not only too old,
but also too attached to his mother.

00:00:38.130 --> 00:00:40.860
Nevertheless, Obi-Wan is allowed to train
Anakin.

00:00:40.860 --> 00:00:45.970
The wider Star Wars canon saw Yoda still concerned
about Anakin's tendency to become emotionally

00:00:45.970 --> 00:00:46.970
attached.

00:00:46.970 --> 00:00:51.300
And in The Clone Wars animated film, Yoda
decided to give Anakin a Padawan of his own

00:00:51.300 --> 00:00:56.280
in Ahsoka Tano, hoping that her eventual ascendancy
to Jedi Knighthood would rob Anakin of this

00:00:56.280 --> 00:00:57.280
tendency.

00:00:57.280 --> 00:00:59.190
But why would he consider that a good idea?

00:00:59.190 --> 00:01:03.829
A Padawan like Ahsoka would get much better
training from someone who wasn't...you know,

00:01:03.829 --> 00:01:05.280
also a Padawan.

00:01:05.280 --> 00:01:09.909
The final arc of the Clone Wars' fifth season
again saw Yoda and Ahsoka at cross purposes.

00:01:09.909 --> 00:01:13.920
When the Jedi Temple was bombed, Anakin and
Ahsoka were called to investigate.

00:01:13.920 --> 00:01:18.030
Although Ahsoka had nothing to do with the
bomb, the true culprit framed her for it.

00:01:18.030 --> 00:01:23.039
After escaping and being recaptured, Ahsoka
was expelled from the Jedi Order by the Council.

00:01:23.039 --> 00:01:27.600
Except, as Yoda admitted at one point in the
storyline, his ability to commune with the

00:01:27.600 --> 00:01:30.729
Force had become clouded due to the events
of the war.

00:01:30.729 --> 00:01:34.869
If that's the case, why was he put in charge
of such a monumental decision?

00:01:34.869 --> 00:01:38.729
Even Padme quickly deduced that Ahsoka couldn't
have committed the crime she was being framed

00:01:38.729 --> 00:01:42.092
for, and she's as far from a Jedi as you can
get.

00:01:42.092 --> 00:01:45.279
If Yoda knew he couldn't see the truth, he
should have stepped aside rather than risk

00:01:45.279 --> 00:01:48.920
unjustly ruining Ahsoka's life.

00:01:48.920 --> 00:01:50.439
Keeping Sith secrets

00:01:50.439 --> 00:01:55.289
In the sixth season Clone Wars episode "The
Lost One," Yoda, Mace Windu and Obi-Wan discover

00:01:55.289 --> 00:01:59.649
the truth of what happened to Sifo-Dyas, the
Jedi Master revealed to have ordered the Clone

00:01:59.649 --> 00:02:02.189
Army's creation in Attack of the Clones.

00:02:02.189 --> 00:02:07.719
The episode shows that decades ago, Sifo-Dyas'
ship was shot down by a crime syndicate paid

00:02:07.719 --> 00:02:09.040
by Count Dooku.

00:02:09.040 --> 00:02:13.440
Dooku then impersonated Sifo-Dyas and ordered
the creation of the clones.

00:02:13.440 --> 00:02:17.540
Yoda decides that the only course of action
the Jedi have is to keep the truth to themselves.

00:02:17.540 --> 00:02:20.760
"Cover up this discovery we must."

00:02:20.760 --> 00:02:23.720
Except that definitely isn't all they could've
done.

00:02:23.720 --> 00:02:27.600
Exposing this betrayal could've resulted in
the withdrawal of the Clone Army, the discovery

00:02:27.600 --> 00:02:32.340
of the inhibitor chip that enabled Order 66,
and a better way out of the Clone Wars.

00:02:32.340 --> 00:02:37.920
Uncovering your enemy's plans, then re-covering
them, is a pretty stupid thing to do.

00:02:37.920 --> 00:02:39.530
A push to the Dark Side

00:02:39.530 --> 00:02:44.450
In Revenge of the Sith, as part of the final
salvo of his plan, Chancellor Palpatine appointed

00:02:44.450 --> 00:02:47.680
Anakin as his special representative on the
Jedi High Council.

00:02:47.680 --> 00:02:49.810
The Council agreed with one condition.

00:02:49.810 --> 00:02:54.510
"You're on this council, but we do not grant
you the rank of Master."

00:02:54.510 --> 00:02:58.070
It was a first in the history of the Council,
and Anakin didn't take it well.

00:02:58.070 --> 00:03:02.010
Even with the rising Dark Side clouding his
vision, Yoda could've surely seen that Anakin

00:03:02.010 --> 00:03:03.680
was at a breaking point.

00:03:03.680 --> 00:03:07.841
Even if Yoda didn't know the details of Anakin's
increasingly unbalanced private life, it was

00:03:07.841 --> 00:03:12.160
obvious that the young Jedi was in crisis
and denying him the rank of Master might have

00:03:12.160 --> 00:03:16.820
been the final straw that pushed him to turn
completely to the dark side.

00:03:16.820 --> 00:03:17.960
Quitting time

00:03:17.960 --> 00:03:22.370
One of the biggest controversies sparked among
Star Wars fans by the prequel trilogy was

00:03:22.370 --> 00:03:27.480
its depiction of Yoda as a highly accomplished
fighter, not just a wise old Jedi Master.

00:03:27.480 --> 00:03:31.190
But even fans who preferred the more sedate
version of the little guy had to admit that

00:03:31.190 --> 00:03:35.570
Yoda's battle with Emperor Palpatine made
for an emotionally charged sequence.

00:03:35.570 --> 00:03:38.860
On the other hand, the end of it all is unbelievably
lame.

00:03:38.860 --> 00:03:43.340
Having realized that they're evenly matched
and he can't really win, Yoda makes an escape.

00:03:43.340 --> 00:03:47.870
But then rather than making any sort of heroic
last stand or plan, Yoda considered the greatest

00:03:47.870 --> 00:03:52.130
Jedi Master of all simply says that he's failed
and has to go into exile.

00:03:52.130 --> 00:03:55.760
Obviously, Yoda had to end up on Dagobah to
set up the original trilogy.

00:03:55.760 --> 00:03:59.750
But that means Yoda also hid himself away
from the Knights he led and who saw him as

00:03:59.750 --> 00:04:04.540
a hero, essentially abandoning all the Jedi
to get purged.

00:04:04.540 --> 00:04:05.870
It's a trap

00:04:05.870 --> 00:04:09.310
While there hasn't been much focus on Yoda
in the new Star Wars canon outside of his

00:04:09.310 --> 00:04:13.600
traditional role, the old Legends continuity
saw him take the spotlight a few times.

00:04:13.600 --> 00:04:18.180
Case in point: Sean Stewart's 2004 novel Yoda:
Dark Rendezvous.

00:04:18.180 --> 00:04:22.000
In that book, set toward the end of the Clone
Wars, Yoda receives a message from Count Dooku

00:04:22.000 --> 00:04:24.260
that he wants to meet to talk about peace.

00:04:24.260 --> 00:04:29.000
Despite knowing full well that this is a trap,
Yoda opts to meet Dooku, taking a pair of

00:04:29.000 --> 00:04:31.010
Knights and their Padawans along with him.

00:04:31.010 --> 00:04:35.060
Almost immediately, the two Jedi Knights are
struck down in an ambush, and Yoda barely

00:04:35.060 --> 00:04:36.600
escapes with his life.

00:04:36.600 --> 00:04:40.630
That's right: the leader of the Jedi walked
knowingly into a trap, and he got two Jedi

00:04:40.630 --> 00:04:41.650
killed for it.

00:04:41.650 --> 00:04:45.570
But that's Yoda for you always the goofball.

00:04:45.570 --> 00:04:46.830
"haha."

00:04:46.830 --> 00:04:47.830
Thanks for watching!

00:04:47.830 --> 00:04:50.139
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:04:50.139 --> 00:04:52.770
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

